(Was previously slib, now xshare - see the new README file)
--------------
Shared Library
--------------


----------------------------------------------------------------------

Introduction and Example
------------------------

   Shared  library is simply  a wrapper library that
simplifies  writing  code that calls  shared/dynamic
libs on both windows and unix systems.  All the code
that calls functions in a windows .dll or a unix .so
won't  have  to be changed when porting between  the
platforms.

   As a simple example, the following windows code:

<code>
   HMODULE lib = LoadLibrary ("mylib.dll");
   FARPROC func = GetProcAddress (lib, "myfunc");
   func ();
   FreeLibrary (lib);
</code>

opens a library  called "mylib.dll",  then calls the 
function "myfunc" within it, then closes the library
when  it is done. The  following unix  code does the
same thing on unix systems:

<code>
   void *lib = dlopen ("mylib.so");
   void *func = dlsym (lib, "myfunc");
   func ();
   dlclose (lib);
</code>

   
   When  porting the  code from one  platform to the
other one would  generally have to locate and change
all the relevant code. Once the changes are complete
then  the changed  code will still  only run on  one
platform.

Using slib, you can do the following instead:

<code>
   slib_library_t lib = slib_open ("mylib");
   slib_symbol_t func = slib_symbol (lib, "myfunc");
   func ();
   slib_close (lib);
</code>

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Usage:
------
   Please read  the header file that comes with  the
slib package for details on what each  function does
and what each function returns.

   There are two main steps to using slib; compiling
and linking. For compilation just follow the example
above and your code should at least compile (you may
need to add  slib.c and slib.h to your build process
or project).

   For linking, the process differs under windows as
compared  to unix-like  systems. In  windows, a .dll 
file is compiled and linked  with no special  linker
or  compiler  support needed while the program which
uses  the .dll needs to be linked in  a certain way.
In a unix system, it's the other way around.

   The  Makefile in the slib package will work under
unix systems  with the  gnu tools and under  windows
with msys and mingw. Briefly, in windows you need to
build a  static (.a)  library using  'ar'  which can 
then be converted to a dll with 'a2dll' (included in
the mingw distribution). In unix the normal flags to
'ld'  will  build the  shared object ('.so') without 
having to  do anything special  (use '-shared' as an
ld flag).

